A||N||6||Wade87GT||Problems with RCC coilovers?||||9||
Z||000000||Wade87GT||12-02-2000||02:03 PM||||I've installed the complete tubular control arm and coilover suspension setup from RCC into my 87 GT. It's now lowered about an inch and using 225/50/16 in front (same as before with factory suspension) and 245/50/16 on the rear the car looks great! During the first test ride the front suspension bottomed out with every crack in the road. Turns out the front coilovers were shipped with the wrong shocks (too long). Not a big deal since RCC shipped the correct replacements right away. Now the front still bottoms out, though not as bad, but still bad enough. With the car on the ground and looking through the coils, the shock has maybe a quarter inch of compression available. Do I still have shocks that are too long? The part number on the Carrera box is 3235. Anybody else out there using this setup who can give me some help? Of course, RCC isn't open on weekends and my car has been off the road for too long.||||reg||9
Z||000001||batboy||12-02-2000||03:19 PM||||I have the rear RCC coil-overs, but not the front. I'd say off hand that the front tires might be too big. When I lowered my front (S/T Sport springs), I had to go with a little bit smaller tires. Also try adjusting the spring up a little, you might be too low for those tires. But most likely, the spring rate you choose is not stiff enough. What spring rate did you get?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wade87GT||12-02-2000||03:46 PM||||Thanks for the tip Batboy, but these are the same size tires I've been using for about 6 years. I've only lowered the car about an inch with this suspension. It appears that the shocks are not allowing any suspension travel. With the car on the ground there's only a quarter inch left of the rod that comes out of the shock body. Raising the car back to stock height would be a bit disappointing since one of the reasons I bought the stuff was to lower the car (I needed a complete suspension rebuild too). Also, the spring rate is 300.||||reg||9
Z||000003||batboy||12-03-2000||02:45 AM||||Well, the front tires I had on the Batmobile worked ok until I lowered it too. However, sound like you really need more shock travel. That set up you're using is used a lot for SCCA racing were they use a higher spring rate. I used to have a Fiero spring rate chart, but can't find it now. Seems like it was posted on the forum about a year ago too. You might try a search.||||reg||1
Z||000004||chrissherman||12-03-2000||03:42 AM||||did you cycle the shocks by pushing them on the floor and leting them pop up on there own  until they pop up all the way farly fast||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||12-03-2000||12:33 PM||||If you can only see 1/4 in of the rod, the shock is too long. Something is not right. Let us know how you make out. I was planning to go with the RCC setup as well.||||reg||1
Z||000006||RNGCRSH||12-27-2000||12:29 AM||||Wade,<P>What was the final outcome with RCC, Did they ship the wrong length coilovers?||||reg||1
